  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B001",
    "B051"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I change my preferred configuration from B001 to B003. The quantum spin Hall achievement remains a fundamental and experimentally mature discovery, but the final discussion has made its attribution boundary more consequential: a citation must remain tightly confined to one prediction and realization without implicitly assigning the broader topological-insulator programme to one experimental representative. B003 has an equally disciplined chain from a distinct physical principle to durable experimental realization. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture has been independently reproduced as an exceptional precision-measurement platform, and Katori and Ye provide a compact, complementary attribution. The public case should rest on the demonstrated quantum-control achievement itself, not on record performance alone or prospective applications.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains essential. Optical lattice clocks depend on a broader optical-frequency-metrology setting, and later international accuracy improvements must not be retroactively assigned to the two proposed laureates. Nor should a citation imply that a redefinition of the second or every proposed fundamental-physics application has already followed. These limitations are manageable only because the proposed citation is restricted to the invention and realization of the lattice-clock architecture.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that optical lattice clocks pair a reproducible new quantum-control regime with unusually clean attribution to Katori and Ye, while keeping the citation separate from downstream applications.",
        "response": "I agree with that boundary and now give it decisive weight. Reproducibility across laboratories is particularly important for an instrument-led physical advance: it shows that the principle has become a robust capability rather than remaining a singular demonstration. I also agree that the prize should recognize the lattice-clock principle and foundational realization, not later performance gains throughout optical metrology."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson maintains that aberration-corrected electron optics opened repeatedly demonstrated sub-angstrom access to materials, but identifies unresolved credit among correction concepts, implementations, and engineering teams.",
        "response": "Her emphasis on experimentally established capability strongly supports B051 as an alternative, especially from a materials-physics perspective. Yet the unresolved allocation is material: the proposed trio must represent both optical concepts and a working instrument amid parallel technical lineages. B003 presently permits a more sharply bounded physical achievement and a more defensible limited attribution."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson cautions that important consequences of optical lattice clocks remain in development and favors inflation because of its observational reach.",
        "response": "The caution is sound and should constrain our wording. I do not regard prospective applications as the rationale for B003; rather, the awardable result is the already realized and reproducible lattice-clock regime. Inflation remains a profound alternative, but its unresolved microphysical origin and unconfirmed distinctive signatures make the present evidentiary boundary less settled than for the narrowly framed clock achievement."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a historical review can demonstrate that Katori and Ye alone fairly delimit the original lattice-clock principle and foundational realization from optical-frequency-comb infrastructure and later international advances.",
      "Whether the quantum spin Hall citation could be narrowed sufficiently to retain Kane, Mele, and Molenkamp as an equally unambiguous alternative.",
      "How to describe the fundamental reach of optical lattice clocks without converting developing applications into claimed results."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
